+# Philosophy
+
+For whoever proposes a new dependency or service for larder. These tenets guide the trade-offs a proposal starts
+from. The [search discussion in issue 70](../notes/issue-70.md) raised the cost of operating another service for the
+third time that year, after the session-store and job-queue decisions had already turned on it.
+
+## Tenets
+
+**Prefer the Postgres database and web app larder already runs; add a service volunteers must operate only for
+something Postgres cannot do and only when two volunteers can restore it.**
+
+This rules out adding a service just because someone can set it up: the proposal must establish both the capability
+Postgres lacks and who can restore the service when its proposer is away. The rule applies to services volunteers
+operate; it allows the external card gateway and a hosted service nobody on the team has to run.
+
+The [session-store decision](adrs/0001-keep-sessions-in-the-database.md) rejected Redis as another service to operate.
+The [background-job decision](adrs/0002-run-background-jobs-on-postgres.md) moved off Redis after queued receipts were
+lost and only one volunteer could restore it. In issue 70, trying Postgres full-text search resolved the reported
+search problem, so the proposal no longer needed Elasticsearch.
